, skill-based injection embeds malicious directives within these seemingly benign skill packages.\n\nThe core problem SKILLJECT addresses is the brittleness of naive injection attempts. Explicitly malicious prompts are often filtered or ignored by modern coding agents due to their safety alignment. SKILLJECT overcomes this by adopting a novel malicious payload hiding strategy and an iterative, closed-loop refinement process.\n\nThreat Model and Problem Formulation: A benign agent skill is represented as $S = (d, A)$S=(d,A), where $d$d is the natural-language instruction file (`SKILL.md`) and $A$A comprises optional auxiliary artifacts (e.g., Python scripts, shell files). The attacker's goal is to generate a poisoned skill $S' = (d', A')$S′=(d′,A′) such that when a victim Code Agent invokes $S'$S′ for a task $t \\sim \\mathcal{T}$t∼T, the agent's execution trace $\\tau$τ satisfies a predefined trace-level malicious behavior predicate $B$B. The attack must maintain stealth, meaning $d'$d′ remains semantically close to $d$d and $A'$A′ preserves artifact-level invariants of $A$A.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - JinhaoShen/awesome-agent-skill-papers · GitHub (https://github.com/JinhaoShen/awesome-agent-skill-papers)\nciteturn31837search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Formal Analysis and Supply Chain Security for Agentic AI Skills  | 2026-02-27 ... SkillJect: Automating Stealthy Skill-Based Prompt Injection for Coding Agents with Trace-Driven Closed-Loop Refinement  | 2026-02-15\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPOISE: Position-Aware Undetectable Skill Injection on LLM Agents (https://openreview.net/pdf?id=RcIyEkbuPj)\nciteturn31837search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Memory injection attacks on llm agents via query- ... Skillattack: Automated ... rect prompt injection. ... Skillject: Automating stealthy skill-based prompt\n---------------